This file gives the gonality of X1(N)/Q for N=1..40, and upper bounds for N <= 300.
It gives explicit proofs for these upper bounds, in the form of a function of that degree.

Other files in this folder http://www.math.fsu.edu/~hoeij/files/X1N/ are:

	*) The file LowDegreePlaces contains non-cuspidal points of degrees d for
	   which we have no function of degree d (e.g. degrees below the Q-gonality).

	*) Subfields: enumerates subfields of Q(X1(N)) over Q(j), for N=1..100,
	   to look for functions of low degree in subfields.

	*) cusp_divisors, cusp_divisors_large, and cusp_divisors_program.
	   These give the divisors of f[2] ... f[floor(N/2)+1],  viewed as
	   functions on X1(N).  The degrees of the places occurring in these
	   divisors are listed in the file:  cusp_degrees.

	   After weighing the entries in a divisor by the corresponding cusp_degrees,
	   the sum of the positive entries will equal the degree of the corresponding
	   function (and the sum of all entries will be zero).

	*) The folder LowerBoundsGonality contains Magma files for proving
	   lower bounds (equal to the upper bounds) for all N <= 40.  The case N=37
	   is treated in a separate sub-folder since it this computation is divided
	   into numerous subcases.

Let

f[2] := b/(16*b^2+(1-20*c-8*c^2)*b+c*(c-1)^3);
f[3] := b;
f[4] := c;
f[5] := b-c;
f[6] := s-1;
f[7] := s-r;
f[8] := r*s-2*r+1;
f[9] := s^2-s-r+1;
f[10] := x-y+1;
f[11] := x^2*y-x*y^2+y-1;
f[12] := x-y;
f[13] := x^3*y-x^2*y^2-x^2*y+x*y^2-y+1;
f[14] := x^2*y-x*y^2-x*y+y^2-1;
f[15] := x^2*y-x^2-x*y^2+x*y-x+y-1;
f[16] := x^2*y^2-2*x^2*y-x*y^3+2*x*y^2-y+1;
f[17] := x^4*y-x^3*y^3-x^3*y+x^2*y^4+x^2*y-x^2-x*y^4+x*y^3-x*y^2+x*y+y^3-2*y^2+y;
f[18] := x^4*y-3*x^3*y^2+x^3*y+2*x^2*y^3+x^2*y^2-2*x^2*y-2*x*y^3+2*x*y^2+y^2-2*y+1;
f[19] := x^5*y^2-3*x^4*y^3+3*x^3*y^4+2*x^3*y^2-2*x^3*y-x^2*y^5-x^2*y^4-x^2*y^3+x^2*y^2+x^2*y+x*y^5-x*y^4+2*x*y^3-2*x*y^2-x*y+x-y^4+2*y^3-y^2;
f[20] := x^4*y-2*x^3*y^2-x^3*y+x^2*y^3+x^2*y^2+2*x^2*y-x^2-2*x*y^2+x*y+y-1;
f[21] := x^5*y^2-3*x^4*y^3-x^4*y^2+3*x^3*y^4+3*x^3*y^3-x^2*y^5-3*x^2*y^4-2*x^2*y^2+3*x^2*y+x*y^5+3*x*y^3-4*x*y^2-y^4+y^3+y-1;
f[22] := x^5*y^2-x^5*y-2*x^4*y^3+2*x^4*y+x^3*y^4+3*x^3*y^3-4*x^3*y^2+x^3-2*x^2*y^4+x^2*y^3+x^2*y^2-x^2*y+x*y^4-x*y^3+x*y^2-x*y-y^3+2*y^2-y;
...

where
	{c = r*s-s, b = r^2*s-r*s},
	{r = b/c  , s = c^2/(b-c)}

	{r = (x^2*y-x*y+y-1)/(x^2*y-x), s = (x*y-y+1)/(x*y)},
	{x = (s-r)/(r*s-2*r+1), y = (r*s-2*r+1)/(s^2-r-s+1)}

and
	f[10] .. f[189] can be downloaded from the website of Andrew V. Sutherland,
	specifically, the first table on: http://math.mit.edu/~drew/X1_altcurves.html

